      "id": 1,
      "text": "Beatty was named after Civil War veteran Montillus Beatty, who became the town's first postmaster in 1905.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatty%2C_Nevada",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1906, the Las Vegas and Tonopah Railroad opened service to Beatty, followed by two other lines in 1907, making it a railroad hub until 1940.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatty%2C_Nevada",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s geodesic dome library is the only library in Nevada housed in such a structure.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatty%2C_Nevada",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Beatty once hosted one of the shortest‑lived gold rushes when the Montgomery Shoshone mine opened in the Panamint Range.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
